title: A shift register
mode: draw
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
parts: dff
---- intro
Chain three flip-flops and a bit moves along them, one place per tick: in at `din`, then `q0`, then `q1`, then `q2`. After three ticks the last three bits that came in are all there, side by side.

That is how a **serial** link, one wire carrying one bit after another, becomes a number you can read all at once.
---- task
Build a three-bit shift register: `din` into the first flip-flop, each one into the next. Show them on `q0`, `q1` and `q2`.
---- start
input din 0 0
clock clk 0 160
led q0 580 0
led q1 580 80
led q2 580 160
---- reference
input din
clock clk
led q0, q1, q2
q0 = dff(din, clk)
q1 = dff(q0, clk)
q2 = dff(q1, clk)
---- answer
input din 0 0
clock clk 0 160
dff f0 140 0
dff f1 280 70
dff f2 420 150
led q0 580 0
led q1 580 80
led q2 580 160
din -> f0.d
f0.q -> f1.d
f1.q -> f2.d
clk -> f0.clk
clk -> f1.clk
clk -> f2.clk
f0.q -> q0
f1.q -> q1
f2.q -> q2
---- hint1
It is the "waiting a tick" circuit with one more flip-flop.
---- hint2
`din` into flip-flop 0, each `q` into the next one's `d` and to its light, and `clk` into all three.
---- solution
Three flip-flops in a row on one clock: a bit put on `din` shows on `q0` after one tick, `q1` after two, `q2` after three.
---- done
Shift registers are everywhere: USB, network cables and the SPI chips on every circuit board send bits one at a time and collect them like this.
